.\" Man page for mount-wrapper — install as /usr/share/man/man1/mount-wrapper.1
.TH MOUNT-WRAPPER 1 "2026-07" "mount-wrapper" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mount-wrapper \- archive auto-mounter orchestrator (ratarmount engines)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mount-wrapper
.I command
[\fIoptions\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mount-wrapper
discovers archives under configured source directories, indexes and mounts them
via external ratarmount engines (default
.BR ratarmount-rs ),
runs first-mount hooks, and exposes a Unix control socket plus an optional
localhost HTTP API and embedded operator SPA.
.PP
It does
.B not
reimplement ratarmount inside Go. Engines, FUSE userland, and optional convert
tools must be installed separately.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B version
Print version, commit, and build date.
.TP
.B help
Show usage summary.
.TP
.B serve
Run the daemon (scan / index / mount / hooks loop). Loads YAML config, opens
the state database, binds the control socket when configured, and optionally
starts the HTTP API when
.B web_enabled
is true.
.RS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " " \fIPATH\fR
Config YAML (default: platform path, e.g.
.IR /etc/mount-wrapper/config.yaml
on Linux).
.TP
.B \-\-once
Run a single service tick then exit (tests / oneshot debug).
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-unauth
Allow control-plane ops without peer credentials. Also enabled when env
.B MOUNT_WRAPPER_CONTROL_ALLOW_UNAUTH=1
.RE
.TP
.B doctor
Offline environment diagnostics (FUSE, bins, paths, disk, peercred notes,
control socket and pidfile liveness, systemd unit when applicable).
Does not require a running
.B serve
process. Live probes never hard-fail offline:
when
.B control_socket
is set, check
.B control_socket_live
warns if the socket is missing, unreachable, or auth-denied (group
.BR mount-wrapper ),
and reports serve version when reachable;
when
.B pid_file
is set, check
.B pidfile_live
warns if the path is missing or the PID is not running;
on Linux with systemd as PID 1, check
.B systemd_unit
best-effort probes
.B systemctl is-active
/
.B is-enabled
for
.B mount-wrapper.service
(warn when inactive or systemctl unavailable).
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine-readable report.
.TP
.B \-\-fix\-systemd
Write a systemd drop-in for source paths (when applicable).
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
With
.BR \-\-fix\-systemd ,
print the generated drop-in unit text (report notes / JSON
.BR details.content )
and do not write the file. Harmless without
.BR \-\-fix\-systemd .
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " " \fIPATH\fR
Config to diagnose.
.RE
.TP
.B config show|set
Show or update configuration.
.RS
.TP
.B show \-\-local
Read the config file only (no control socket).
.TP
.B set
Apply a full config or shallow patch via the running daemon, or offline with
.BR \-\-dry\-run .
Flags:
.BR \-\-json ,
.BR \-\-file ,
.BR \-\-patch ,
.BR \-\-dry\-run .
.RE
.TP
.B status
Service status (requires
.BR serve ).
Default stdout is a multi-line human summary.
.B \-\-json
for machine-readable output;
.B \-\-sizes
includes size metrics (slower).
With
.B \-\-sizes
and without
.BR \-\-json ,
human status is followed by a sizes appendix (summary totals + per-archive
archive/index/extracted/space-saved lines). Combine
.B \-\-sizes \-\-json
for the full status JSON including
.B metrics_summary
and per-row
.BR metrics .
.TP
.B metrics [\fIARCHIVE_ID\fR]
Archive size metrics (requires
.BR serve ).
Default stdout is multi-line human output: a summary block (when listing all
archives) plus per-archive size lines.
.B \-\-json
prints the control payload as machine-readable JSON.
.B \-\-no\-cache
bypasses the metrics TTL cache;
.B \-\-prefer\-mount
walks the FUSE mount for extracted size instead of the index first.
Index-first and prefer-mount results are cached separately.
Optional
.I ARCHIVE_ID
limits the report to one archive.
.TP
.B rescan
Trigger an immediate source scan (requires
.BR serve ).
Default stdout is a one-line human scan summary
.RB ( seen ,
.BR inserted ,
.BR stable ,
etc.);
.B \-\-json
prints the control scan summary.
.B \-\-assume\-stable
bypasses the stable-file gate (admin/acceptance only).
.TP
.B retry \fIARCHIVE_ID\fR
Reset mount attempts for one archive (requires
.BR serve ).
Default stdout is a human line
.RB ( archive_id ,
.BR status ,
.BR mount_attempts );
.B \-\-json
for the control archive dict.
.TP
.B mount \fIPATH\fR
Request mount of an archive path (requires
.BR serve ).
Default stdout is a human line (queued or started with
.BR pid / mount_path );
.B \-\-json
for the control payload.
.TP
.B unmount \fITARGET\fR|\-\-all
Unmount managed archive(s) (requires
.BR serve ).
Default stdout is a human summary (single target or
.B \-\-all
list);
.B \-\-json
for the control payload.
.TP
.B purge \fIARCHIVE_ID\fR
Purge state/index/overlay for an archive. Requires
.B \-\-yes
and a running
.BR serve .
Default stdout is a human purge summary;
.B \-\-json
for the control payload.
.TP
.B hooks list|status|rerun
Inspect or re-run first-mount hooks (requires
.BR serve ).
.B hooks list
lists discovered
.I hooks.d
scripts (human multi-line by default;
.B \-\-json
for control payload);
.B hooks status \fIARCHIVE_ID\fR
shows per-archive hook rows (human multi-line by default;
.B \-\-json
for control payload);
.B hooks rerun
.RI [ \-\-force ]
.RI [ \-\-json ]
.I ARCHIVE_ID
invokes control op
.B hooks_run
.RB ( \-\-force
bypasses terminal hooks_status eligibility;
.B \-\-json
for machine output). Put flags before
.IR ARCHIVE_ID .
Without
.BR \-\-force ,
eligibility matches serve:
none|pending|retry|running, and failed only when
.B hook_rerun_on_failure
is set. Archive lifecycle must be
.B mounted
or
.BR hooks_running .
.TP
.B reload
Reload configuration from disk (control
.B reload
op / SIGHUP equivalent). Applies hot-reloadable keys such as
.BR log_level ,
.BR source_dirs ,
and
.BR use_inotify
(inotify watches are restarted). Requires a running
.BR serve .
Default stdout is the human line
.BR "reload scheduled" ;
.B \-\-json
prints the control ack as machine-readable JSON (for example
.BR {"reload":"scheduled"} ).
.TP
.B stop
Request graceful
.B serve
shutdown (control
.B stop
op / SIGTERM equivalent). Sets the stop flag; the serve loop exits and runs
.BR Shutdown
(unmounts, closes sockets). Requires a running
.BR serve .
Default stdout is the human line
.BR "stop scheduled" ;
.B \-\-json
prints the control ack as machine-readable JSON (for example
.BR {"stop":"scheduled"} ).
.SH COMMON OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " " \fIPATH\fR
Config YAML path.
.TP
.BR \-\-socket " " \fIPATH\fR
Control socket path (overrides
.B control_socket
in config) for socket-backed CLI commands.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Primary file:
.I /etc/mount-wrapper/config.yaml
(Linux FHS). Schema version 1. Hot-reloadable keys apply on
.B reload
/ SIGHUP without a full process restart;
.BR web_enabled ,
.BR web_token ,
.BR web_host ,
.BR web_port ,
and path keys require process restart (HTTP bind and token are fixed at serve start).
See packaged examples under
.I /usr/share/mount-wrapper/
or the source tree
.IR packaging/examples/ .
.PP
Important security-related keys:
.TP
.B web_enabled / web_host / web_port / web_token
HTTP API + SPA. Default bind is loopback. Empty
.B web_token
means no Bearer auth (acceptable only on trusted loopback).
Changing any of these four keys requires a process restart.
When the web server is on,
.B GET /metrics
exposes Prometheus text gauges (lifecycle counts, low_disk, last scan, info,
and aggregate size/savings totals from metrics_summary — no per-archive series).
Default scrapes request status with include_sizes and may be slower than
count-only status. Scrapes on loopback need no token.
.TP
.B log_level
Daemon slog verbosity (DEBUG, INFO, WARNING, ERROR, CRITICAL). Hot-reloaded.
Overridden while
.B MOUNT_WRAPPER_LOG_LEVEL
is set to a valid level.
.TP
.B control_socket
Unix socket path for CLI and local control.
.TP
.B hooks_dir
Directory of first-mount hook executables (must pass owner/writable/realpath
checks).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MOUNT_WRAPPER_CONTROL_ALLOW_UNAUTH
When set to
.BR 1 ,
allows control connections without peer credentials (default off). Prefer
fix/group socket auth in production.
.TP
.B MOUNT_WRAPPER_LOG_LEVEL
When set to a valid level (DEBUG, INFO, WARNING, ERROR, CRITICAL), overrides
config
.B log_level
for the serve process at start and on each config reload. Invalid values are
ignored. See
.I packaging/env.example
for a sample EnvironmentFile.
.TP
.B WSL_DISTRO_NAME
Used by the HTTP
.B /api/wsl-info
helper for Windows UNC path hints.
.PP
Hook processes receive
.B MOUNT_WRAPPER_*
variables only for archive and config fields (no dual
.B TARMOUNT_*
export from this daemon). Engine-specific variables such as
.B RATARMOUNT_*
may still be set for child mount processes.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/mount-wrapper/config.yaml
Main configuration.
.TP
.I /etc/mount-wrapper/hooks.d/
First-mount hooks.
.TP
.I /var/lib/mount-wrapper/
Mounts, indexes, overlays, state database.
.TP
.I /run/mount-wrapper/control.sock
Default control socket (mode 0660).
.TP
.I /run/mount-wrapper/
Runtime directory (socket + pidfile).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
General error (config load/validation failure, command failure, or other
control/protocol error not mapped below).
.TP
.B 2
Usage error (unknown command, invalid flags, or missing required arguments).
.TP
.B 4
Service unavailable (control socket missing, not a live listener, or
.B serve
not running).
.TP
.B 5
Permission denied (control-plane peer credentials rejected; caller is not
root or a member of the
.B mount-wrapper
group, and unauth is not enabled).
.SH SECURITY
Control socket authentication uses peer credentials (root or group
.BR mount-wrapper ).
The HTTP API defaults to loopback with optional Bearer token. Destructive HTTP
actions (purge, unmount-all, rescan) are rate-limited per client. See
.I docs/security.md
in the source distribution for a full review of hooks path escape, token
defaults, and escape hatches.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fusermount3 (1),
ratarmount / ratarmount-rs documentation.
.PP
